<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-algebra-factorisation">
  <title>Factorisation</title>
  <section xml:id="algebra-factorisation-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>To factorise an expression is to rewrite it as a product. Factorisation reverses expansion. A common factor can be taken outside brackets; other patterns require recognising how products expand.</p></introduction>
    <p>Always check a factorisation by expanding the result. A correct answer must reproduce every term of the original expression.</p>
    <p>For a paired expansion and factorisation investigation, begin with <xref ref="af3-01-positive-factors-unit-factor"/>. Recognise the same structure in both directions before moving to more general trinomials.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="af3-factorisation-revisit">
<title>AF3-01: Revisit, Recognise, Reverse</title>
<objectives><title>Learning Intentions</title><ol><li><p>Revisit expansion to recognise a useful factorisation pattern.</p></li><li><p>Distinguish a sum middle coefficient from a product-plus-one middle coefficient.</p></li><li><p>Check a factorisation by expanding.</p></li></ol></objectives>
<paragraphs xml:id="af2-factorisation-signpost"><title>Curriculum signpost — AF2: Algebraic expressions</title><p><em>Interpret, substitute, understand equality.</em> Return to familiar expressions with a new purpose. Recognise the coefficients, check selected numerical values and explain why the factorised expression is equal to the original for every permitted input. AF3 concerns the transformation itself; AF7 provides a graphical connection.</p><p>Revisit the <xref ref="af2-expansion-signpost" text="title"/> for the original curriculum connection.</p></paragraphs>
<p>We have already studied expansion. Now we revisit it with fresh examples and a different purpose: to recognise how a trinomial may have been formed. Refer to <xref ref="af3-01-positive-factors-unit-factor"/> for the original investigation and its three representations.</p>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-friends-twenty"><title>Revisit expansion — friends of twenty</title><statement><p>Expand <m>(9x+11)(x+1)</m>.</p></statement><solution><me>(9x+11)(x+1)=9x^2+9x+11x+11=9x^2+20x+11.</me><p>Notice the sum <m>9+11=20</m>. Both cross-products have a unit coefficient multiplier.</p></solution></example>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-reverse-thirty"><title>Recognise and reverse — friends of thirty</title><statement><p>Factorise <m>13x^2+30x+17</m>.</p></statement><solution><p>Since <m>13+17=30</m>, recognise the sum-middle case.</p><me>13x^2+30x+17=(13x+17)(x+1).</me><p>Expand to check all three coefficients.</p></solution></example>
<example xml:id="af3-revisit-two-arrangements"><title>Same exterior coefficients, different middles</title><statement><p>Expand <m>(5x+7)(x+1)</m> and <m>(5x+1)(x+7)</m>. Then use your observations to factorise <m>3x^2+11x+8</m> and <m>3x^2+25x+8</m>.</p></statement><solution><me>\begin{aligned}(5x+7)(x+1)&amp;=5x^2+12x+7,\\(5x+1)(x+7)&amp;=5x^2+36x+7.\end{aligned}</me><p>The middle coefficients are <m>5+7=12</m> and <m>5\cdot7+1=36</m>.</p><me>\begin{aligned}3x^2+11x+8&amp;=(3x+8)(x+1),\\3x^2+25x+8&amp;=(3x+1)(x+8).\end{aligned}</me><p>In the first, <m>3+8=11</m>. In the second, <m>3\cdot8+1=25</m>. Both are recognition strategies, not universal rules for factorising every trinomial.</p></solution></example>
<p>Use the <xref ref="af3-yardsticks-interactive-figure"/> to investigate the same relationship with your own numbers. The <url href="https://ronandownes.github.io/apps/apps/algebra/af3-01-unit-factor/index.html" visual="inline, display and area-model app"/> lets you revisit the original unit-factor case and then switch to factorisation.</p>
<exercise xml:id="af3-revisit-paired-practice"><title>Practise in both directions</title><statement><p>Expand the first three expressions and factorise the last three. Identify which pattern applies before calculating.</p><ol><li><p><m>(19x+11)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(x+1)(cx+d)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(4x+1)(x+9)</m></p></li><li><p><m>17x^2+100x+83</m></p></li><li><p><m>px^2+(p+q)x+q</m></p></li><li><p><m>6x^2+43x+7</m></p></li></ol></statement><answer><ol><li><p><m>19x^2+30x+11</m></p></li><li><p><m>cx^2+(c+d)x+d</m></p></li><li><p><m>4x^2+37x+9</m></p></li><li><p><m>(17x+83)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(px+q)(x+1)</m></p></li><li><p><m>(6x+1)(x+7)</m></p></li></ol></answer></exercise>
<paragraphs xml:id="af3-revisit-learning-to-learn"><title>Learning to Learn — Learning to Teach</title><p>Revisiting a familiar operation is valuable when we have a new purpose. Here, expansion helps us see how a factorisation might be reconstructed. New numbers and letters test whether we recognise the structure rather than remember an answer. Explain your recognition strategy to someone else, and check it by reversing the operation.</p></paragraphs>
</section>
<section xml:id="algebra-factorisation-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-algebra-factorisation">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Factorise 6x+9.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>The highest common factor is 3, so 6x+9=3(2x+3).</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-algebra-factorisation">
      <statement><p>Factorise x²+7x+12.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>(x+3)(x+4).</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
